'\"
.TH "TclTk9Upgrade" 3tcl 0\&.5\&.0 "rbc" "Upgraded Tcl/Tk9\&.0-ready RBC package"
.SH NAME
TclTk9Upgrade - Commands in namespace TclTk9Upgrade
.SH "TCL/TK9\&.0 UPGRADE"
.SH "TCL/TK 9 MODERNIZATION"
.PP
The original Rbc codebase was designed around Tcl/Tk 8\&.x APIs and data-size assumptions\&. Although an earlier
port made Rbc build with Tcl/Tk 9, this fork has undergone a much broader modernization to use Tcl/Tk 9
interfaces correctly, remove obsolete compatibility infrastructure, improve 64-bit safety, and harden the
graphing code against numerical, memory, and platform-specific edge cases\&.
.PP
The modernization covers the following areas\&.
.SS "TCL 9 COMMAND AND OBJECT API"
.TP
\(bu
Converted command implementations to the Tcl 9 object-based command interface\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated command callbacks to use Tcl_Size argument counts where appropriate\&.
.TP
\(bu
Migrated command creation to Tcl_CreateObjCommand2() for Tcl 9 large-argument support\&.
.TP
\(bu
Replaced remaining old string-based command and result handling with Tcl_Obj-based APIs\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed obsolete command parsing infrastructure inherited from the original BLT/Rbc implementation\&.
.TP
\(bu
Reworked operation dispatch tables and argument validation around object-based parsing\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved const-correctness throughout Tcl-facing APIs and removed casts that previously discarded const\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed several local copies or emulations of Tcl internal parsing structures and functions\&.
.SS "64-BIT AND TCL_SIZE SUPPORT"
.PP
A large part of the original code assumed that collection sizes, vector lengths and indexes fit in an
int\&. These assumptions were systematically removed\&.
.TP
\(bu
Converted vector lengths, indexes and capacities to Tcl_Size\&.
.TP
\(bu
Converted spline point counts and interpolation indexes to Tcl_Size\&.
.TP
\(bu
Converted graph element point indexes and data-index mappings to Tcl_Size\&.
.TP
\(bu
Converted error-bar index mappings and element-to-data mappings to Tcl_Size\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated sorting, searching, closest-point and line-reduction code for large indexes\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated chain/list traversal code where sizes or indexes could exceed the old 32-bit assumptions\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed vector binary-reading and vector mathematical operations for Tcl 9 size semantics\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added overflow checks before converting between Tcl_Size, size_t, integers and allocation sizes\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added allocation-overflow guards for dynamically sized arrays throughout graph, spline and vector code\&.
.PP
This removes a large class of silent truncation and overflow problems that were possible with large datasets\&.
.SS "MODERN TK OPTION HANDLING"
.PP
The graph subsystem previously relied heavily on the legacy Tk configuration API\&. Most of it has been replaced
with the modern Tk option-table interface\&.
.PP
The following subsystems were migrated to Tk_OptionSpec/Tk_OptionTable based configuration:
.TP
\(bu
graph widgets
.TP
\(bu
axes
.TP
\(bu
legends
.TP
\(bu
grid
.TP
\(bu
crosshairs
.TP
\(bu
pens
.TP
\(bu
markers
.TP
\(bu
line elements
.TP
\(bu
stripchart elements
.TP
\(bu
bar elements
.TP
\(bu
PostScript options
.PP
Legacy configuration tables and their associated processing functions were removed after the new
implementations were activated\&.
.PP
Custom option types were added or rewritten for complex Rbc values such as:
.TP
\(bu
vectors
.TP
\(bu
axes
.TP
\(bu
pens
.TP
\(bu
tags
.TP
\(bu
element styles
.TP
\(bu
states
.TP
\(bu
coordinates
.TP
\(bu
padding and margins
.TP
\(bu
format strings
.TP
\(bu
graph-specific enumerations
.PP
Configuration processing was also made transactional where needed\&. Invalid option changes now preserve the
previous valid object state instead of leaving partially modified graph objects\&.
.PP
The EPS canvas item remains on Tk_ConfigSpec/Tk_ConfigureWidget() because the public Tk 9 Tk_ItemType
canvas-item interface still exposes the legacy configuration mechanism\&.
.SS "GRAPH OBJECT AND RESOURCE LIFETIME"
.PP
Configuration modernization exposed a number of ownership and rollback problems that were corrected at the same
time\&.
.TP
\(bu
Reworked creation, configuration and destruction paths for graph components\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved rollback after failed configurations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected ownership of dynamically allocated option values\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed temporary Tk image creation and destruction\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected resource cleanup for legends, markers, pens and elements\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened vector mathematical operations against ownership and aliasing problems\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed dead initialization and cleanup paths inherited from the old configuration system\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed substantial amounts of obsolete and unreachable compatibility code\&.
.SS "AXIS AND COORDINATE-SYSTEM MODERNIZATION"
.PP
The axis implementation received extensive numerical and behavioral cleanup\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened coordinate mapping against integer overflow and extreme coordinate ranges\&.
.TP
\(bu
Widened intermediate geometry calculations before converting to Tk/X11 integer coordinates\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added safe handling for rotated-axis dimensions\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added validation for non-finite configuration values\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened generated tick calculations against invalid or degenerate ranges\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added guards against ill-defined tick spacing and numerical overflow\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected axis scrolling and -scrollcommand behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected tick-label command callback handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved option validation and rollback for axis configuration\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened user-controlled formatting options and removed unsafe formatting operations\&.
.PP
Logarithmic axes were also substantially corrected:
.TP
\(bu
Logarithmic axes now use well-defined positive-domain semantics\&.
.TP
\(bu
Invalid/non-positive points are excluded instead of being mapped through invalid logarithms\&.
.TP
\(bu
Line elements are split at invalid log-domain points instead of drawing across discontinuities\&.
.TP
\(bu
Bar charts correctly handle logarithmic axis mapping\&.
.TP
\(bu
Stacked bars were corrected for logarithmic scales\&.
.TP
\(bu
Extreme logarithmic ranges are handled more safely\&.
.TP
\(bu
Smoothing no longer bridges invalid log-domain gaps\&.
.SS "LINE, BAR AND ELEMENT PROCESSING"
.PP
The internal representation and processing of graph elements were extensively updated\&.
.TP
\(bu
Modernized element option handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Reworked vector association using object-based APIs\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated element state, tag, pen, style and axis handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed error-bar mapping and data-index tracking\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected closest-point and closest-segment calculations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected element binding/tag behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed line-reduction routines and their index handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened construction of mapped point arrays and drawing traces\&.
.TP
\(bu
Replaced internal uses of narrow X11 rectangle structures where larger internal coordinates were required\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added explicit handling of NaN and infinite values in plotted data\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected bar handling of non-finite data\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected several element formatting and -valueformat paths\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed step-generation behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected line -trace behavior\&.
.SS "SPLINE SUBSYSTEM"
.PP
The spline subsystem was subsequently extended with direct complex-vector parametric interpolation\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added spline parametric natural source result samples\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added spline parametric quadratic source result samples\&.
.TP
\(bu
Parametric source and result vectors are first-class complex Rbc vectors\&.
.TP
\(bu
Parametric splines use cumulative two-dimensional chord length and do not require either Cartesian coordinate to be monotonic\&.
.TP
\(bu
Vertical paths, reversing paths and loop-like trajectories are supported\&.
.TP
\(bu
Consecutive duplicate source points are rejected because they create a zero-length parameter interval\&.
.TP
\(bu
Parametric generation is transactional and supports source/result aliasing\&.
.TP
\(bu
The original scalar real-vector spline interface remains unchanged\&.
.SS "VECTOR SUBSYSTEM"
.PP
Vector handling was updated substantially beyond the minimum required for compilation with Tcl 9\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added Tcl 9 sized vector indexing\&.
.TP
\(bu
Converted sorting indexes to Tcl_Size\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed global state from vector sorting operations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated vector arithmetic and mathematical operations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected vector duplication and overwrite behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected binary-read operations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened allocation and capacity calculations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved parsing and const-correctness\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed legacy parser dependencies\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved ownership of temporary data used by mathematical operations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added stricter handling of invalid and non-finite input\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added first-class complex vectors, complex expression arithmetic and mathematical functions, and typed public C API access while retaining compatibility with the existing real-vector interface\&.
.SS "GEOMETRY, TEXT AND DRAWING"
.PP
Many drawing routines inherited assumptions from old X11 APIs where coordinates and dimensions were commonly
stored in narrow integer types\&.
.PP
The modernization:
.TP
\(bu
widened internal geometry calculations;
.TP
\(bu
added checked conversion at native drawing boundaries;
.TP
\(bu
hardened rotated text and bounding-box calculations;
.TP
\(bu
updated text-layout processing;
.TP
\(bu
hardened shadows, halos and graph-layout coordinate arithmetic;
.TP
\(bu
corrected clipping and projection calculations;
.TP
\(bu
updated polygon, rectangle and region processing;
.TP
\(bu
added guards around fixed-size polygon and symbol allocations;
.TP
\(bu
corrected point-in-region and projection helpers;
.TP
\(bu
fixed drawing-boundary issues discovered on both Windows and Linux\&.
.PP
The intent is to keep high-precision or wide internal calculations for as long as possible and narrow them only
when calling APIs that inherently require integer coordinates\&.
.SS "IMAGE AND BITMAP HANDLING"
.PP
The image-processing code was significantly updated for current Tk behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated photo-image access and processing\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected temporary image lifetime management\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated bitmap rotation and scaling code\&.
.TP
\(bu
Unified bitmap-region operations across platforms\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected Unix image handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected Windows bitmap representation and mask generation\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened bitmap allocation and size calculations\&.
.TP
\(bu
Isolated native bitmap-handle access behind Rbc Windows helper functions\&.
.SS "POSTSCRIPT OUTPUT"
.PP
The PostScript subsystem was modernized and hardened alongside the graph code\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated PostScript option handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated image generation and bitmap handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected PostScript writer behavior\&.
.TP
\(bu
Replaced unsafe formatting paths\&.
.TP
\(bu
Hardened formatting and buffer handling\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated coordinate and size handling for the modernized graph structures\&.
.SS "WINDOWS DRAWING BACKEND"
.PP
The Windows drawing implementation received a substantial cleanup\&.
.PP
Obsolete compatibility code and dead drawing implementations were removed, and the remaining native drawing
code was reorganized behind an Rbc-owned Windows abstraction\&.
.PP
Rbc code outside the Windows backend no longer accesses Tk's private Windows drawable structures directly\&.
.PP
Operations such as:
.TP
\(bu
acquiring and releasing a drawable HDC;
.TP
\(bu
extracting an HBITMAP from a Tk pixmap;
.TP
\(bu
wrapping an HBITMAP as a pixmap;
.TP
\(bu
wrapping a native HDC;
.TP
\(bu
mapping an HWND to the required drawable representation;
.TP
\(bu
setting Windows GDI raster operations
.PP
are now isolated behind Rbc_Win* helper functions\&.
.PP
This reduced the former widespread dependency on:
.TP
\(bu
TkWinDrawable
.TP
\(bu
TkWinBitmap
.TP
\(bu
TkWinDC
.TP
\(bu
TkWinDCState
.TP
\(bu
TkWinGetDrawableDC()
.TP
\(bu
TkWinReleaseDrawableDC()
.TP
\(bu
TWD_*
.PP
to a single Windows implementation unit, rbcWinDraw\&.c\&.
.SS "REMAINING PRIVATE TK DEPENDENCIES"
.PP
Complete elimination of private Tk interfaces is currently not practical without either changes in Tk itself or
a significant drawing-backend redesign\&.
.PP
The remaining dependencies are deliberately isolated:
.TP
\(bu
rbcText\&.c uses Tk's internal TkDrawAngledChars() functionality for rotated text\&. Tk does not currently provide equivalent functionality through its public API\&.
.TP
\(bu
rbcWinDraw\&.c uses tkWinInt\&.h to bridge between Tk drawables and native Windows GDI objects\&.
.PP
No other graph, element, image or general Rbc code needs to know about Tk's Windows-private drawable structures\&.
.PP
Compiler diagnostics caused by known attribute mismatches inside the private Tk headers are suppressed only
around those specific includes; warnings remain enabled for Rbc's own source code\&.
.SS "C SAFETY AND HARDENING"
.PP
The Tcl/Tk 9 work also included a wider C safety audit\&.
.TP
\(bu
Replaced unsafe sprintf() usage with bounded or object-based formatting\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added validation for user-controlled numeric formatting strings\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added multiplication and allocation-overflow checks\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added range checks before narrowing floating-point or wide integer values\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added explicit handling of NaN and infinity\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed undefined or questionable integer-overflow paths\&.
.TP
\(bu
Improved const correctness\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed mutations through pointers to constant data\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed uninitialized index/data-mapping cases\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed obsolete K&R-era and compatibility code where encountered\&.
.TP
\(bu
Removed dead #ifdef notdef and otherwise unreachable implementations where they no longer served a purpose\&.
.SS "BINDINGS AND TAGS"
.PP
The graph binding infrastructure was corrected and expanded during the migration\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed axis bindings\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed element bindings\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed marker bindings\&.
.TP
\(bu
Fixed legend binding activation\&.
.TP
\(bu
Corrected graph tag generation and lookup\&.
.TP
\(bu
Updated bind-tag handling after the option-system migration\&.
.TP
\(bu
Added regression tests for the affected binding paths\&.
.SS "USER-VISIBLE ADDITIONS"
.PP
In addition to the Tcl/Tk 9 modernization and correctness work, this fork adds several user-visible features that were
not present in the original Rbc implementation\&.
.PP
\fBComplex vectors\fR
.PP
The vector subsystem now supports both real and complex numeric vectors\&. Complex vectors are created explicitly
with -type complex, and Tcl-level complex values use the form {real imag}:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
::rbc::vector create z -type complex
z set {{1 2} {3 -4} {5 0}}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Complex vectors are supported by most vector instance operations and by the vector expression engine, including
arithmetic, complex exponentiation, trigonometric and logarithmic functions, projections such as real,
imag, abs, and arg, and complex statistical reductions\&.
.PP
Operations that require an ordering of values remain real-only\&. In particular, sort and normalize are not
supported for complex vectors\&.
.PP
Traditional graph, barchart, and stripchart X/Y element data remain real-valued\&. Polar elements can attach a
complex vector directly through -cdata, and the spline subsystem provides complex-vector parametric natural
and quadratic interpolation\&.
.PP
Applications can still explicitly derive real projections such as real, imag, abs, or arg when a
traditional Cartesian graph or scalar spline is required\&.
.PP
The public C API was extended with typed vector access, including Rbc_VectorType, Rbc_Complex,
Rbc_CreateVectorWithType(), Rbc_VectorComplexData(), Rbc_ResetComplexVector(), and
Rbc_VectorChanged()\&. The original real-vector API remains available for compatibility\&.
.PP
\fBPolar and Smith charts\fR
.PP
This fork adds the polar graph widget for plotting Cartesian complex-plane data with either a conventional
Polar grid or a Smith-chart representation\&.
.PP
Polar element data remain Cartesian: X is the real coordinate and Y is the imaginary coordinate\&. Elements may
use ordinary X/Y data or attach first-class complex vectors directly with -cdata\&.
.PP
Complex data can be interpreted as:
.TP
\(bu
reflection coefficient with -cdataformat gamma;
.TP
\(bu
physical impedance with -cdataformat impedance;
.TP
\(bu
physical admittance with -cdataformat admittance\&.
.PP
The -z0 option specifies the positive reference impedance used for impedance/admittance conversion\&.
.PP
Smith charts support impedance, admittance, or combined contour grids, configurable major/minor normalized
contour values, and Tcl callbacks for formatting real and imaginary contour labels\&.
.PP
Polar grids provide independently configurable angular major/minor spokes, angular label callbacks, radial
label formatting through the mapped X axis, and configurable label anchoring\&.
.PP
Polar and Smith grid geometry follows the axes selected by grid -mapx/-mapy\&.
.PP
Unless -aspect is explicitly configured, Polar widgets automatically maintain equal physical scale for X and
Y data units\&. This keeps circles circular even when the mapped numerical X/Y ranges have different spans\&.
.PP
Polar grid rendering remains meaningful when the origin is outside the current viewport\&. Radial circles which
intersect the viewport are retained, angular spokes are clipped to the visible Cartesian area, and angular
labels move from the normal circular layout to the visible ends of their spokes when no complete centered
circle is available\&.
.PP
Radial labels remain tied to the positive zero-degree ray and are displayed whenever their corresponding
{radius,0} point is visible, even when the Polar origin itself is outside the viewport\&.
.PP
Polar autoscaling also supplies a neutral fallback range when a grid-mapped axis has no contributing visible
element data, allowing the Polar grid to remain usable when the widget is empty or all plotted elements are
hidden\&.
.PP
Smith reactive labels likewise adapt to zoomed views\&. When the complete unit circle is no longer visible,
major reactance and susceptance labels are moved to the first visible boundary of their corresponding contour
instead of disappearing with their normal unit-circle label position\&.
.PP
Natural/cubic and quadratic smoothing on Polar elements is parametric, allowing vertical paths,
non-monotonic-X paths and loops\&. Catmull-Rom smoothing remains parametric as well\&.
.PP
Missing, non-finite, logarithmically invalid, or singular impedance/admittance points split a trace into
continuous runs\&. Smoothing is performed independently on those runs and never bridges a gap\&.
.PP
element closest on a Polar widget additionally reports radius and angle\&. In Smith representation it also
reports Gamma together with normalized and physical impedance and admittance quantities\&.
.PP
Polar/Smith grid rendering, labels, custom formatter callbacks and smoothed element traces are supported by
PostScript output as well as on-screen drawing\&.
.PP
\fBPlot-only element hiding\fR
.PP
Line, stripchart, and bar elements now support the -hideplot option\&.
.PP
Unlike -hide, which removes an element from the graph display list, -hideplot yes suppresses plotting while
leaving the element in the display list\&. Its legend entry therefore remains available, making it possible for
applications to hide and restore plotted data without rebuilding or reordering the legend\&.
.PP
A plot-hidden element is also excluded from automatic axis limits, closest-element searches, element hit
testing, and PostScript element output\&. Plot-hidden bars are additionally excluded from bar grouping and
stacking calculations\&.
.PP
-hide and -hideplot are independent, so changing the normal visibility of an element does not alter its
-hideplot state\&.
.PP
\fBArrowheads for line markers\fR
.PP
Line markers now support arrowheads through the -arrow and -arrowshape options\&.
.PP
-arrow accepts none, first, last, or both, allowing an arrowhead to be drawn at either or both ends
of a line marker\&. -arrowshape {a b c} controls the arrowhead geometry using screen distances\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g marker create line -name direction -coords {0 0 5 4 10 2} -outline red -linewidth 2 -arrow last

\&.g marker configure direction -arrow both -arrowshape {8 10 3}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Arrowheads use the line marker's -outline color, and the line shaft is shortened to the arrowhead neck so
that it does not extend through the filled arrowhead\&.
.PP
The drawing implementation was also corrected for X11/Linux\&. Arrowheads are concave polygons, so they are now
passed to the native polygon renderer with the correct non-convex geometry and without the duplicate closing
vertex\&. This keeps arrow rendering consistent across supported platforms\&.
.PP
\fBBar geometry from element closest\fR
.PP
The element closest operation now provides additional geometry information when the selected element is a bar\&.
.PP
In addition to the normal name, index, x, y, and dist entries, the result array contains:
.TP
\(bu
left
.TP
\(bu
top
.TP
\(bu
right
.TP
\(bu
bottom
.PP
These values are the physical widget coordinates of the final mapped bar rectangle that is actually drawn\&. They
therefore reflect the current bar mode: stacked bars return the selected stacked segment, while aligned and
overlapping bars return the adjusted rectangle belonging to the selected element\&.
.PP
The values remain physical widget coordinates when -invertxy is enabled: left and right always describe
physical X coordinates, while top and bottom always describe physical Y coordinates\&.
.PP
This makes element closest directly usable for bar highlighting, overlays, annotations, and interactive
interfaces without requiring applications to reconstruct bar geometry themselves\&.
.SS "REGRESSION TESTING"
.PP
The modernization was accompanied by a substantial expansion of the test suite\&.
.PP
New regression coverage was added for areas including:
.TP
\(bu
option configuration and rollback;
.TP
\(bu
axes and axis bindings;
.TP
\(bu
markers;
.TP
\(bu
element options and bindings;
.TP
\(bu
legends;
.TP
\(bu
vectors and vector arithmetic;
.TP
\(bu
splines;
.TP
\(bu
non-finite values;
.TP
\(bu
logarithmic axes;
.TP
\(bu
bar charts;
.TP
\(bu
PostScript output;
.TP
\(bu
bitmap and image handling;
.TP
\(bu
formatting options;
.TP
\(bu
error bars;
.TP
\(bu
closest-point calculations;
.TP
\(bu
coordinate and integer-boundary cases\&.
.PP
Manual graph tests and demos were also repeatedly exercised during the migration\&.
.PP
The resulting code has been tested on both Windows and Linux with Tcl/Tk 9\&.x\&.
.SS RESULT
.PP
The current codebase is no longer merely an older Rbc implementation adjusted until it compiles with Tcl/Tk9\&.0\&.
The major subsystems now use Tcl/Tk 9-sized interfaces and modern Tk configuration machinery, large-dataset
index handling is based on Tcl_Size, numerical and allocation boundaries are explicitly checked, and the
unavoidable private Tk dependencies are confined to narrow platform/rendering interfaces\&.
.PP
The remaining use of legacy or private Tk interfaces is intentional:
.TP
\(bu
the EPS canvas item uses the configuration API still required by Tk's public custom-canvas-item interface;
.TP
\(bu
rotated text uses Tk's unsupported angled-text API;
.TP
\(bu
the Windows drawing adapter uses Tk's private drawable representation\&.
.PP
These remaining interfaces can only be removed cleanly if equivalent public Tk APIs become available or if the
relevant rendering code is replaced with an independent backend\&.


